  "worked_solution": "For sufficiently large negative values of $x,$ we have $|6x-4|=-(6x-4)=-6x+4.$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|6x-4|}{3x+4}={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-6x+4}{3x+4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-6x+4}{3x+4} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-6x}{x}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-6+\\frac{4}{x})}{(3+\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6+0}{3+0} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
